WEBVTT

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:05.230
Satoshi, who aims to battle
the Supreme Monarch, Dande...

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:10.240
And Go, who dreams of catching every Pokémon
to work his way up to Mew...

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.530
This is the story of
their lives and adventures.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.500
There it is!

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.170
Okido Laboratory!

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.130
Professor Okido!

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.330
Race ya there!

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.550
Wait for me!

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.930
Okido Laboratory, Kanto Region

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.930
My, it has been a while.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.650
Have you two been well?

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:39.690
Yeah!

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.770
Go, I don’t believe I’ve seen you
since the Sakuragi Institute inauguration event.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:47.930
Yeah, and we didn’t get
a good chance to talk that day.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.400
Oh, here are some documents
Director Sakuragi had me bring.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.650
Ooh! Sorry for the bother.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.130
Go, is this your first time here?

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.770
No, I came here for a Pokémon Camp
when I was six.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.590
That reminds me, we were all held up
because someone was late.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.800
Yeah, some people are like that!

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.560
Guess they overslept or something...

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.250
Huh? Wait a sec...
Oversleeping... When you were six...

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:15.430
Maybe it was you all along, Satoshi.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:20.230
On that day I went to
Pokémon Camp, I met Mew.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.640
And that’s what inspired my dream!

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.040
Hmm? What sort of dream?

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.780
To reach Mew, which is said
to have the genes of all Pokémon...

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.770
...by catching every Pokémon!

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.400
Ah, that’s a marvelous dream.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.240
How many Pokémon
have you caught so far?

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.990
H-Have a look!

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:44.080
My, my, this is quite impressive.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:45.870
Suicune?!

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.860
Yeah! I caught it!

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:50.460
Very impressive!

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.820
Ah, that’s right...
Go, have you heard of Project Mew?

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.310
Project...

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.050
...Mew?

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:04.020
Yes. A team to meticulously search for Mew
is being set up, and they’re recruiting.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.360
But you must complete all sorts of Trial Missions
if you wish to become a member.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.560
Huh.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.130
What do you think?

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.030
If you’re interested,
I can recommend you.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.400
Thanks for the offer...

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.200
...but I have to pass.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.320
What?!

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.930
Why, Go?

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.140
I don’t really like being
part of a team...

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.900
Those missions or whatever
don’t sound appealing, either...

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:28.970
Hmm...

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:33.410
I thought it would present a good opportunity
for you to meet trainers sharing your dream.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.170
Well, I won’t twist your arm.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.550
Oh, right, Satoshi.
Your Pokémon have been waiting to see you.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.850
Project Mew, huh?

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:29.290
Lizardon! Fushigidane!

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.420
I haven’t seen you in ages!

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.420
Everyone!

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:42.810
I’ve missed you!

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.680
Huh, so those are
Satoshi’s Pokémon.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.310
Kentauros!

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:53.090
Stop!

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:53.940
Satoshi?!

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:00.830
Betobeton!

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.310
A-Are you okay?!

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.160
Yeah! Totally fine!

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.200
Glad to see you all
look so happy!

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.700
There was one point of concern,
however...

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.120
“Point of concern”?
What is it?

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.650
I haven’t seen Goukazaru
for the last two or three days.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:22.610
Huh?

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.220
Is that one of your Pokémon?

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.090
Yeah.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.090
I’ve raised him since he was a Hikozaru.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.410
I was worried when he
failed to turn up for meals,

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:34.780
so Kenji and I looked for him,
but we couldn’t find him anywhere.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.030
I wonder why...

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:41.830
Professor Okido, did Goukazaru
do anything unusual recently?

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:43.230
Hmm...

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:48.380
Now that you mention it...
Before he disappeared, he battled Lizardon.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:53.610
And before that, he battled
Magmarashi, Chaoboo, and Fiarrow, as well.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.580
Those are all Fire types.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.930
Was he training or something?

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.890
That wouldn’t be a reason
to leave though, yeah?

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.160
For now, I’ll go look for him!

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:03.960
I’ll come with!

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:11.560
Goukazaru!

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.610
Say something if you can hear me!

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.580
I guess he’s not here...

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.440
Up there!

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.060
Huh? Where?

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.400
Look, up there!

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:28.280
Pidgeon! Bird Pokémon!
Normal/Flying type!

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.310
Really..?

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.110
Yes, really!

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:34.490
I’ve tried and failed to catch
lots of Pidgeon!

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.720
Monster Ball, go!

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:50.180
Failed again!

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:54.710
I’m not giving up!

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:03.870
A-An Iwark!

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.940
What a stroke of luck!
Monster Ball, go!

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:19.710
Kamex! Hydro Pump!

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:27.910
W-We’re safe...

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:30.140
But isn’t that Kamex...?

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:36.010
Of course,
who else could it be but Satoshi?

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:37.710
Shigeru!

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:41.440
I haven’t had a chance
to say that in a while.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.030
Long time no see,
Satoshi and Pikachu.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.890
Yeah, it’s been forever!

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.070
But why are you here?

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:52.030
Weren’t you supposed to be
researching Pokémon in Sinnoh?

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:54.750
That’s old news.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:58.580
Now, I’m traveling all over the world
to research Pokémon.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:02.830
And, while traveling,
something sprung up that brought me to Kanto.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:04.240
What was it?

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.040
Well, never mind that...

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.050
Aren’t you going to introduce us?

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:09.340
Oh, sorry!

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.560
He’s a research fellow
at Sakuragi Institute, just like me.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:14.510
I’m Go.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:15.960
Hmm...

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.560
That’s a cute Sarunori.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:22.560
This is Shigeru.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.080
A childhood friend,
and longtime rival!

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.050
Nice to meet you.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:29.570
Likewise.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.360
Oh, right! Shigeru’s grandpa
is Professor Okido!

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.510
Huh?! Professor Okido?!

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:39.210
I was intrigued when I heard
Satoshi’d found himself a buddy.

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:41.490
Wondered what kinda guy he was.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.540
“What kinda guy”...?
I’m just who I am.

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:48.040
It’s not your personality
I’m interested in.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:52.710
What I want to know
is how skilled a Pokémon Trainer you are.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.460
If I’m supposed to accept
that Satoshi has a buddy,

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.540
he’d better be
at least as good a trainer as me.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.060
...What do you mean?

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.190
I’m saying you’re not qualified
to be Satoshi’s buddy.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:07.490
Gaaaah!

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:10.110
Satoshi! What’s wrong
with this guy?!

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.800
Oh, he always did
this sorta stuff to me too...

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.990
By the way, Shigeru,
what was that thing you mentioned?

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:19.990
The Legendary Pokémon, Fire.

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:21.700
Fire?

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:26.950
I analyzed the temperature, humidity, wind direction,
and past appearances,

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:30.670
and judged that Fire
is likely to appear...

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.420
...in a valley near here.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.750
Huh, that’s amazing!

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.470
I could have done that,
too, you know...

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:38.430
So why are you here, Satoshi?

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:43.930
Oh, my Goukazaru,
who I left at Okido Laboratory, went missing...

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:45.770
Your Goukazaru, huh...

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:49.240
I remember seeing him battle
in the Sinnoh League.

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:52.090
Do you have any clues?

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:55.410
Seems before he left,
he battled Lizardon.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:59.140
Magmarashi, Chaoboo,
and Fiarrow, too.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:00.610
I see.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:05.010
Perhaps Goukazaru
went to the valley I’m heading to.

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:07.310
There’s no time to waste.
Let’s go.

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:08.220
Yeah.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.870
It’s a pretty dangerous place.
Will you be able to keep up?

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:14.300
Of course!

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.340
It’s not <i>you</i>
I’m concerned about.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:21.470
If Satoshi can do it,
then so can I!

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:23.630
Don’t disappoint me, now!

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.430
Hey, what sort of adventures
have you had since Sinnoh?

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:29.390
No mere rundown
could ever do them justice.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:30.890
What about you?

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:35.530
Me? I, uh...
I’ve done loads!

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.410
Pocket Monsters!

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.430
Look at this.

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:13.660
This valley was formed from magma
that once flowed through here.

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:15.280
Huh.

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:17.440
Go, do you notice anything?

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:23.560
In other words,
this used to be a volcanic area.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:26.590
The perfect sort of place
for Fire to appear.

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:27.810
Correct.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:29.740
But your analysis missed something.

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:30.640
Huh?

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:36.060
Fire. Flame Pokémon.
Fire/Flying type.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:41.630
If it is injured, it is said to dip its body in the
molten magma of a volcano to burn and heal itself.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:43.490
And one more thing.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:47.760
It is said that its appearance
indicates the coming of spring.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:48.720
Spring?

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:51.140
Correct.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.820
Flowers have bloomed early here.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:58.190
Combining that with some other data,
I narrowed down the time and place it would appear.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:00.900
But I now have a new piece of data.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.010
Satoshi, your Goukazaru.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:05.410
Goukazaru?

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:11.430
I’ve seen that Goukazaru many times
when visiting Okido Laboratory.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:15.510
He was always working hard
to become stronger.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:18.460
So if that Goukazaru was aiming
for ever greater heights,

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:20.550
and left to challenge
someone to a battle...

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:24.580
His desired opponent may be
the Legendary Fire-type, Fire.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.720
Go, have you figured out
what I’m saying yet?

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:32.100
Since Goukazaru’s also a Fire type,

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.060
he sensed that Fire would appear here.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:37.120
What? Really?!

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:39.980
So if we were to find Goukazaru here,

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:43.180
it would be even more likely
that Fire will appear.

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:46.590
That voice...

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.040
He’s calling for Fire.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:56.380
Goukazaru!

00:12:57.680 --> 00:12:59.550
We were worried about you!

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.840
You look like you’re doing good...

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:10.140
Did you come here to battle Fire?

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:13.020
Look, this is Fire.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:18.770
Shigeru, you called it!

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.160
Oh, I called more than just that.

00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:22.830
Have a look!

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.450
It looks like it’s responding
to Goukazaru’s wish.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:39.200
Fire!

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:50.110
Yeah, I get how you feel!

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:54.280
Fire! Battle us!

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:02.190
Let’s go, Goukazaru!
Flamethrower!

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:17.370
Fire Spin?!

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:18.250
Dig!

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:22.500
Mach Punch!

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:25.840
It dodged?

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.110
That’s Air Slash!

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:35.630
Don’t give up, Goukazaru!

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:36.600
Flare Drive!

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:46.990
Goukazaru!

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:52.210
You still wanna fight, don’t you?

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:52.870
But..

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:54.570
Satoshi, look out!

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.480
Let’s go!

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:02.760
Hydro Pump!

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:16.470
Rapid Spin!

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:24.100
Rocket Headbutt!

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:29.140
I knew I couldn’t block it completely...

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:33.120
So I used the recoil from the collision
for a Rocket Headbutt!

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.490
Now! Water Pulse!

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:41.870
Amazing...

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:49.760
That move...
It couldn’t be...

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:53.890
Is that Burn Up?!

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.920
Kamex!
Meet it with Hydro Pump!

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:16.120
What’s Burn Up?!

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.780
A move that burns up
all the fire in your body.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:24.010
The user won’t be
Fire type anymore.

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:25.750
Won’t be
Fire type anymore?

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:27.750
That can happen?!

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:30.630
Pokémon biology
is an incredibly complex field of study.

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:38.900
It’s using the Flying-type move
Hurricane?

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:40.520
Defend with Rapid Spin!

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:49.190
Kamex!

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:54.010
You battled well,
considering that <i>Fire</i> was your opponent.

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:55.280
Have a nice, long rest.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:16:59.660
Me next!

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.910
Aceburn! Pyro Ball!

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:12.670
Elecible! Thunder!

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:12.670
Pikachu, 100,000 Volts!

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:43.980
Shigeru!
Fire’s getting away!

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:45.060
That’s fine.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:45.830
What’s that?

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:48.160
A feather from Fire.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:50.250
This was my objective.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.190
It was, huh?

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:55.360
Go, did you get a video of Fire?

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.720
I was sure I pressed
the Record button...

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:01.260
That’s not like you, Go...

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.270
You can’t rush things.

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:05.140
Mind your own business!

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:08.520
All right...

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:10.400
Got it!

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:12.980
What’s that...?

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:16.100
An Armorgaw. I called for it.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:22.240
Look after it.

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:42.350
Everyone was worried about you!

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:47.300
At any rate, I’m surprised
it went to challenge Fire to a battle.

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:48.810
I was shocked, too!

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:52.820
Battling Fire was so much fun!

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:55.690
And I’m glad you got
what you wanted, Shigeru...

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:57.960
Huh? Where’s Shigeru?

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:01.830
Did you receive the Fire feather?

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:04.450
Yes. I’m just verifying it now.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:06.550
Who’s he talking to?

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:10.070
To tell you the truth,
Shigeru is taking part in Project Mew.

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:10.990
Huh?

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:12.660
Then, does that mean...?

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:13.630
Yes.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:18.090
It looks like his Trial Mission
was to acquire a Fire feather.

00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:21.490
I’ve verified the Fire feather.

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.530
Good work, as always.

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:25.350
I’ll send you the token, then.

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:32.310
And I’ve received it. See you.

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:34.570
Shigeru? What’s a token?

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:39.320
You get them for clearing
Trial Missions.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:40.110
I see.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:44.620
Go, I hear you declined
an invitation to Project Mew.

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:48.240
That’s a wise decision!

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:52.370
A trainer at your level
could never pass the missions.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:57.500
Well then, I’ll be off again,
Grandfather.

00:19:57.500 --> 00:20:00.280
I mean... Professor Okido.
See you next time.

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:01.310
Right.

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:04.650
Take care out there.
And come visit if you’re ever nearby.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:08.140
I will. See you, Satoshi.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.140
Shigeru...
Where are you going now?

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:16.400
I’m aiming to research
everything to do with Pokémon.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:22.650
To do so means I need to research Mew,
who holds the genes of all Pokémon.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:24.770
I’ve made up my mind.

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:29.600
Even if my goal seems impossible,
I’ll press on ahead without giving up.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:32.040
I get it. I’ll cheer you on!

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:34.620
Thank you. And see you.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.750
Everything about Pokémon, huh...?

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:40.990
He’s as cool as ever!

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:44.030
I’ll do it.

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.390
Project Mew!

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:51.390
Go! I’ll cheer you on
with everything I’ve got, too!

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:53.430
Thank you, Satoshi!

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:55.900
Yes. Shigeru will be pleased.

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:57.770
Huh? Why?

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:02.860
Provoking you was likely
his way of expressing...

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:06.390
...that he’d like you to take part.

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:08.300
Then he should’ve just said that!

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:10.580
He can never be honest
about how he feels...

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:17.630
I... kind of feel like he
completely beat me.

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:21.230
Either way,
there’s one thing I now know!

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:24.080
Right now, I can’t beat him!

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:26.010
So I’m going to try harder!

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:30.300
And then,
the future will be in my hands!

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:34.930
Go has decided to participate
in Project Mew.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:39.710
What sort of trials
await him in the future?

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:09.630
Now! Onto the next adventure!

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:10.820
Go!

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:17.250
In that case, how about asking
Sarunori and Eevee to run an errand?

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:20.290
Sarunori...
I know you can do it!

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:22.960
Eevee...
It’s all up to you!

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:26.640
That’s a pretty big road!

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.950
Will they really be okay?

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.010
Next time, on Pocket Monsters:

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:33.780
I Want to Watch Their First Errand!
